{"schemaVersion":1,"key":"fundamentals/rag","track":"engineers","lang":"en","dir":"ltr","title":"RAG & Embeddings","objectives":{"lang":"en","items":["Explain why retrieval beats fine-tuning for facts that change","Describe how text becomes an embedding and how similarity is measured","Walk through the retrieve-then-generate pipeline end to end","Measure retrieval with Recall@k, Precision@k, MRR and nDCG@k","Decide, for a given problem, whether you want RAG or fine-tuning"]},"prerequisites":[{"key":"fundamentals/llms","number":1,"title":"LLM Fundamentals","drawn":true,"track":"engineers","lang":"en"},{"key":"fundamentals/training","number":2,"title":"Training LLMs","drawn":true,"track":"engineers","lang":"en"}],"previous":{"key":"fundamentals/training","number":2,"title":"Training LLMs","drawn":true,"track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"next":{"key":"fundamentals/tools","number":4,"title":"Tool Calling","drawn":true,"track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"toc":[{"id":"why-rag-exists","text":"Why RAG Exists","depth":2},{"id":"the-desktop-and-the-library","text":"The Desktop and the Library","depth":3},{"id":"what-rag-does","text":"What RAG Does","depth":2},{"id":"vector-embeddings","text":"Vector Embeddings","depth":2},{"id":"vector-databases","text":"Vector Databases","depth":2},{"id":"the-rag-process","text":"The RAG Process","depth":2},{"id":"measuring-retrieval","text":"Measuring Retrieval","depth":2},{"id":"a-worked-example","text":"A Worked Example","depth":3},{"id":"tools-for-rag","text":"Tools for RAG","depth":2},{"id":"why-not-just-fine-tune-the-model-on-your-documents","text":"Why Not Just Fine-tune the Model on Your Documents?","depth":2},{"id":"key-terms","text":"Key Terms","depth":2},{"id":"summary","text":"Summary","depth":2},{"id":"references","text":"References","depth":2},{"id":"-articles","text":"📄 Articles","depth":3},{"id":"-videos","text":"🎬 Videos","depth":3},{"id":"️-tools","text":"🛠️ Tools","depth":3},{"id":"️-repositories","text":"🗃️ Repositories","depth":3}],"body":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/llms","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"LLM Fundamentals"}]},{"type":"text","text":" gave you the context window. It is the model's working desk, and everything on it has to fit. "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/training","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Training LLMs"}]},{"type":"text","text":" gave you fine-tuning, which changes the model itself. This module is about the third option. It is also the option you will use most often."}],"lead":true},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"why-rag-exists","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Why RAG Exists"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"You have useful data:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"your codebase"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"your company's documents"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"a folder of contracts"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"years of support tickets"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"There is far more of it than fits in a context window. And the context window is a hard limit."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"But this is the important point: "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"for any one question, you do not need all of it."}]},{"type":"text","text":" Somebody asks about the termination clause in one vendor contract. That person does not need the other two hundred contracts. They need one clause."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"So that is the whole idea. Keep the data outside the model. When a question comes in, fetch only the parts that answer it, and put those parts in the context. "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation"}]},{"type":"text","text":" is the name for this: retrieve first, then generate."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"the-desktop-and-the-library","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The Desktop and the Library"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Here is a simple way to think about this."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The model's weights are a library."}]},{"type":"text","text":" It is enormous, and everything the model learned in pre-training is in there somewhere. "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The context window is your desktop."}]},{"type":"text","text":" It is small, and whatever you put on it is right in front of you."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Remembering something from a huge library is hard and unreliable. Reading a page that is open on the desk in front of you is easy and exact."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"RAG is choosing which pages to put on the desk."}]},{"type":"image","number":"3.1","title":"The naive RAG flow","note":"The basic \"naive\" RAG flow. The query goes to two places at the same time. One path goes through an embedding model, to search the vector store index. The other path goes straight to the LLM, together with the context that the search returned.","src":"course-images/fundamentals/rag.webp","alt":"The naive RAG flow","width":875,"height":489},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"what-rag-does","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"What RAG Does"}]},{"type":"diagram","number":"3.2","title":"What RAG Does","svg":{"light":"api/v1/diagrams/3ddac1b0a507d307ec5101a33903638e.svg","dark":"api/v1/diagrams/38f798e4f4efd3198b953fa6bf59e958.svg"}},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The same trick works for anything made of text, not only code. Say you have a folder of legal contracts. Somebody asks \"what is the termination notice period in the Acme vendor contract?\". The model does not guess from memory. Instead, RAG searches across every contract you have and finds the clause that answers the question. Then it gives the model only that clause."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"This is the mechanism underneath. An encoder model turns text into vector embeddings. You keep those embeddings in a vector database. Then you turn the question into a vector too, and you find the closest matches."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"vector-embeddings","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Vector Embeddings"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"An embedding is a list of numbers that describes a piece of text. Think of it as a fingerprint. A special model, called an encoder, produces it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The useful property is this: "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"similar meaning gives similar numbers."}]},{"type":"text","text":" It works like addresses in a city, where close addresses mean nearby places. We measure how close two fingerprints are with "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"cosine similarity"}]},{"type":"text","text":". A high score means the two are very similar."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The classic picture of this uses four words:"}]},{"type":"image","number":"3.3","title":"King, queen, man and woman as vectors in space","note":"Four words become four vectors, and those vectors become positions in space. \"king\" lands near \"queen\", and \"man\" lands near \"woman\". Now look at the two arrows. They are parallel, so the step from king to queen is the same move as the step from man to woman. In this way, the meaning of the words has turned into distances and directions in space.","src":"course-images/fundamentals/vector-king-queen.webp","alt":"King, queen, man and woman as vectors in space","width":1400,"height":468},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Look at that parallel for a second. It shows that the numbers are more than a lookup table, which only gives each word its own fixed entry. The embedding did more than place similar things together. It also placed the "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"relationship"}]},{"type":"text","text":" between them in a consistent direction."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Here is one honest simplification. The picture draws three dimensions so that it fits on a page. Real embeddings have hundreds or thousands of dimensions. That is exactly why we measure closeness with cosine similarity, instead of looking at it."}]},{"type":"diagram","number":"3.4","title":"Vector Embeddings","svg":{"light":"api/v1/diagrams/b7291cd98955f22eb9352cfbcce62f23.svg","dark":"api/v1/diagrams/48af78f4a1d4e8f060352212b7fb379b.svg"}},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Here are two different functions, and their numbers come out close together. That closeness is the whole point. It is what lets a search find one function when you ask for the other."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The important part is that this catches "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"meaning"}]},{"type":"text","text":", not matching words:"}]},{"type":"diagram","number":"3.5","title":"Vector Embeddings","svg":{"light":"api/v1/diagrams/9cbe99635acc4cdc88f9c7fe4de5e300.svg","dark":"api/v1/diagrams/0fd28452f159a51590922c31545d10fe.svg"}},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The two clauses have the same meaning and almost no words in common. The vectors still land next to each other."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"That is why keyword search would miss Clause B, and retrieval finds it."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"vector-databases","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Vector Databases"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"A vector database stores millions of these fingerprints. It keeps each one linked to its original text, and it searches them fast. You give it a query vector, and it returns the top N closest ones. Then you pass those to the LLM."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Free and local:"}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"ChromaDB"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the easiest one to start with."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Milvus"}]},{"type":"text","text":": for bigger projects."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"FAISS"}]},{"type":"text","text":": very fast in-memory search."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Managed services:"}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Pinecone"}]},{"type":"text","text":": simple and hosted."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Weaviate"}]},{"type":"text","text":": good when your data has a structure that you want to keep."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-rag-process","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The RAG Process"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Setup, done once and then again whenever the data changes:"}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Load your source data: code files, contracts, policies, PDFs, any text."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Break it into small chunks, such as a function, a paragraph or a contract clause."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Turn each chunk into an embedding."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Store the embeddings in a vector database."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Answering, done for each question:"}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Take the user's question."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Turn it into an embedding."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Search the database for the closest chunks."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Get the real text of those chunks."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Put that text into the context, together with the question."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The LLM answers from what it can now see."}]}]}]},{"type":"image","number":"3.6","title":"RAG offline and online","note":"The offline half runs once and again whenever the documents change; the online half runs for every question. The picture adds one step the lists leave out: a reranker reads the 20 closest chunks and keeps the best 3, so the context holds only what answers the question.","src":"course-images/fundamentals/rag-offline-online.webp","alt":"RAG offline and online","width":1076,"height":481},{"type":"diagram","number":"3.7","title":"The RAG Process","svg":{"light":"api/v1/diagrams/1ba6bbc01aadd9407c343af4c509a361.svg","dark":"api/v1/diagrams/690c83dc6def479406af422a86ed4707.svg"}},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Replace \"code chunks\" with \"contract clauses\", and the flow is exactly the same."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"measuring-retrieval","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Measuring Retrieval"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The answer can only be as good as the chunks that reach the desk. If retrieval misses the right chunk, the model never sees it. So before you judge the answers, measure the retrieval on its own."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"To do that, you need a small test set. It is a list of questions, and for each question, the chunks that a person marked as relevant. You run retrieval for each question and look at the top "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"k"}]},{"type":"text","text":" results. Here, k is the number of chunks you keep (for example, 5). Four measures are common:"}]},{"type":"table","number":"3.1","title":"Measuring Retrieval","rowHeaders":false,"align":[null,null],"head":[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Measure"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The question it answers"}]}],"rows":[[{"inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Recall@k"}]}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Of all the relevant chunks, how many are in the top k?"}]}],[{"inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Precision@k"}]}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Of the top k chunks, how many are relevant?"}]}],[{"inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"MRR"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (mean reciprocal rank)"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"How high is the "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"first"}]},{"type":"text","text":" relevant chunk?"}]}],[{"inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"nDCG@k"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (normalized discounted cumulative gain)"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Are the relevant chunks near the top, in the best order?"}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"a-worked-example","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"A Worked Example"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Take the question from the example above, \"how does the add function work?\", and give it to a weaker retriever that returns five chunks. Two chunks are relevant: "},{"type":"code","text":"def add"},{"type":"text","text":" and the test for "},{"type":"code","text":"add"},{"type":"text","text":". This retriever puts them second and fourth:"}]},{"type":"table","number":"3.2","title":"A Worked Example","rowHeaders":true,"align":[null,null,null,null,null,null],"head":[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Position"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"1"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"2"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"3"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"4"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"5"}]}],"rows":[[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Chunk"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"code","text":"def multiply"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"code","text":"def add"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"code","text":"def subtract"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"code","text":"test_add"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"code","text":"def divide"}]}],[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Relevant?"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"no"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"yes"}]}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"no"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"yes"}]}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"no"}]}]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Recall@5 = 2 / 2 = 1.0."}]},{"type":"text","text":" Both relevant chunks are in the top 5. For RAG, this is usually the first number to watch, because a chunk that is not retrieved can never be used."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Precision@5 = 2 / 5 = 0.4."}]},{"type":"text","text":" Three of the five chunks are noise. They still go on the desk, where they take up space and can distract the model."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Reciprocal rank = 1 / 2 = 0.5"}]},{"type":"text","text":", because the first relevant chunk is at position 2. MRR is the average of this number over all the questions in the test set."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"nDCG@5 = 0.65."}]},{"type":"text","text":" Each relevant chunk scores less the lower it sits: the score of a relevant chunk at position p is divided by log2(p + 1). Here that gives 1/log2(3) + 1/log2(5) = 0.63 + 0.43 = 1.06. The best possible order, with both relevant chunks at the top, would give 1 + 0.63 = 1.63. And 1.06 / 1.63 = 0.65. A score of 1.0 means a perfect order. nDCG can also give some chunks more weight than others, when a person grades them as \"very relevant\" or \"a little relevant\"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Recall and precision only count what is in the top k. MRR and nDCG also care about the order. A reranker, like the one in the figure above, moves the best chunks to the top. That raises MRR and nDCG, and when you keep fewer chunks, it raises precision too. Weaviate's "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://weaviate.io/blog/retrieval-evaluation-metrics"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Evaluation Metrics for Search and Recommendation Systems"}]},{"type":"text","text":" gives the formula for each of the four, and code for most of them."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"tools-for-rag","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Tools for RAG"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"You will almost never write the retrieval loop yourself. Pick a level, and let something else do the rest:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://www.llamaindex.ai/"},"inlines":[{"type":"logo","src":"course-images/fundamentals/logos/llamaindex.svg","alt":"","mono":false},{"type":"text","text":"LlamaIndex"}]}]},{"type":"text","text":": the highest level. Point it at a folder, ask a question, and get an answer. It handles chunking, embedding, storing and retrieving."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://haystack.deepset.ai/"},"inlines":[{"type":"logo","src":"course-images/fundamentals/logos/haystack.svg","alt":"","mono":false},{"type":"text","text":"Haystack"}]}]},{"type":"text","text":": build the pipeline from ready-made parts. Use it when you want to see and change each step."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/chroma-core/chroma"},"inlines":[{"type":"logo","src":"course-images/fundamentals/logos/chroma.svg","alt":"","mono":false},{"type":"text","text":"ChromaDB"}]}]},{"type":"text","text":": a vector database that stores your text next to its embeddings. So a search gives you back the original chunk."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/facebookresearch/faiss"},"inlines":[{"type":"logo","src":"course-images/fundamentals/logos/meta.svg","alt":"","mono":false},{"type":"text","text":"FAISS"}]}]},{"type":"text","text":": the search layer and nothing else. It is very fast, and it knows only about numbers."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Here is FAISS. It is the smallest thing worth showing, because it is the raw mechanism that every option above wraps:"}]},{"type":"code","language":"python","lines":[[{"text":"import","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" faiss"}],[{"text":"import","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" numpy "},{"text":"as","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" np"}],[],[{"text":"index "},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" faiss."},{"text":"IndexFlatL2(","role":"function"},{"text":"128","role":"number"},{"text":")","role":"function"},{"text":"                              # 128-dimension vectors","role":"comment"}],[{"text":"index."},{"text":"add(np.random.random((","role":"function"},{"text":"100","role":"number"},{"text":", ","role":"function"},{"text":"128","role":"number"},{"text":")).astype(","role":"function"},{"text":"'float32'","role":"string"},{"text":"))","role":"function"},{"text":"   # your 100 chunk embeddings","role":"comment"}],[],[{"text":"query "},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" np.random."},{"text":"random((","role":"function"},{"text":"1","role":"number"},{"text":", ","role":"function"},{"text":"128","role":"number"},{"text":"))","role":"function"},{"text":"."},{"text":"astype(","role":"function"},{"text":"'float32'","role":"string"},{"text":")","role":"function"},{"text":"        # the question, embedded","role":"comment"}],[{"text":"distances, indices "},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" index."},{"text":"search(query, ","role":"function"},{"text":"5","role":"number"},{"text":")","role":"function"},{"text":"                 # the five nearest chunks","role":"comment"}]]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"That is the search step, and only the search step. FAISS returns positions. So it is your job to keep the original text somewhere and to look it up by index. The tools above exist to save you from that bookkeeping. They store the text with the vector, and they give you the chunk itself."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"If you prefer to start from a complete RAG system instead of building one yourself, these open-source projects are ready to use:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/infiniflow/ragflow"},"inlines":[{"type":"logo","src":"course-images/fundamentals/logos/ragflow.svg","alt":"","mono":false},{"type":"text","text":"RAGFlow"}]}]},{"type":"text","text":" is a full RAG platform with a web interface. You run it with Docker. It is good at reading messy documents, including layouts, tables and scanned pages. Its answers carry citations back to the source."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/HKUDS/LightRAG"},"inlines":[{"type":"logo","src":"course-images/fundamentals/logos/lightrag.svg","alt":"","mono":false},{"type":"text","text":"LightRAG"}]}]},{"type":"text","text":" builds a knowledge graph of the people, things and relations in your documents. It keeps embeddings beside that graph. So a search can follow connections, not only similarity. It is a Python library with a server and a web UI. It is also a lighter alternative to Microsoft's GraphRAG."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/VectifyAI/PageIndex"},"inlines":[{"type":"logo","src":"course-images/fundamentals/logos/pageindex.svg","alt":"","mono":false},{"type":"text","text":"PageIndex"}]}]},{"type":"text","text":" uses no vector database and no chunking. It builds a tree of each document, like a table of contents. Then an LLM reasons its way down the tree to the right section. Its argument is that a passage that is similar to the question is not always the passage that answers it."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/HKUDS/RAG-Anything"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"RAG-Anything"}]}]},{"type":"text","text":" is built on LightRAG. It handles documents that mix text with images, tables and equations. Since 2026, LightRAG itself includes this ability too."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/arc53/DocsGPT"},"inlines":[{"type":"logo","src":"course-images/fundamentals/logos/docsgpt-mono.svg","alt":"","mono":true},{"type":"text","text":"DocsGPT"}]}]},{"type":"text","text":" is a self-hosted app. It turns your documents, websites and connected apps into assistants, and those assistants answer with cited sources."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/HKUDS/MiniRAG"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"MiniRAG"}]}]},{"type":"text","text":" is a research project. It makes RAG work with small language models, so it can run on a single device."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/StarTrail-org/PixelRAG"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"PixelRAG"}]}]},{"type":"text","text":" searches screenshots of pages instead of the text taken out of them. This way, tables, charts and layout are not lost. It comes from a 2026 research paper, and it is very new."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"why-not-just-fine-tune-the-model-on-your-documents","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Why Not Just Fine-tune the Model on Your Documents?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"You learned about fine-tuning in "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/training","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Training LLMs"}]},{"type":"text","text":", so this is the obvious question. Why bother with embeddings and a database at all?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Because your company's knowledge is alive, and fine-tuning is a snapshot."}]},{"type":"text","text":" Your codebase gets new commits every day. Contracts get changed, new ones get signed, and policies change. Fine-tuning takes hours or days, and it costs real money. You cannot run it again every time a file changes. Fine-tuning a model to hold your company's documents is solving a live problem with a photograph."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Because fine-tuning is for tasks, not for data."}]},{"type":"text","text":" It is good at teaching a model a "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"behaviour"}]},{"type":"text","text":": summarise like this, always answer in this format, classify into these categories. It is bad at teaching a model "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"facts"}]},{"type":"text","text":". Your hundred pages land among billions of parameters, and those parameters are full of everything else the model ever read. So a fine-tuned model can easily confuse your details with something it half-remembers from pre-training."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Because you are in a race against a moving target."}]},{"type":"text","text":" Suppose you spend a month fine-tuning a model on your task. By the time you finish, the next frontier model is out. A lab trained it with resources that you do not have. Even without any fine-tuning, it is probably better at your task than your fine-tuned model from the last generation. You cannot win that race. RAG does not take part in it, because the same retrieval works with whichever model is the newest."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Because retrieval puts the answer in front of the model."}]},{"type":"text","text":" Go back to the desktop and the library. Fine-tuning tries to put your documents on a shelf somewhere in a library of billions. Then it hopes that the model remembers the right shelf. RAG puts the one page you need on the desk, open, at the moment someone asks the question."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Because a RAG answer can be checked."}]},{"type":"text","text":" With RAG, you know exactly which chunks the model read, so the app can cite them next to the answer. A user can open the source and verify the answer, and when an answer is wrong, you can see which chunks the model had when it went wrong. A fine-tuned model gives you none of this. Its knowledge is mixed into the weights, and it is very hard to say which training document produced a given answer. "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/memory","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Memory"}]},{"type":"text","text":" comes back to this difference."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"In one line: "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"fine-tuning changes what the model "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"knows"}]},{"type":"text","text":"; RAG changes what the model "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"sees"}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"One more point: this is not a choice of one or the other. You can fine-tune a model for how it should behave, and use RAG for what it should know. That combination is common in production."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"key-terms","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Key Terms"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Context window"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the limit on the text a model handles at one time, which is the model's working desk."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Fine-tuning"}]},{"type":"text","text":": training that changes the model itself, and whose result is a snapshot of your data on one day."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)"}]},{"type":"text","text":": a method that keeps your data outside the model, fetches the relevant parts for each question, and generates the answer from them."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Retrieval"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the search step that finds the chunks that answer a question."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Reranker"}]},{"type":"text","text":": a step that reads the closest chunks, for example 20, and keeps only the best few, for example 3."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Chunk"}]},{"type":"text","text":": a small piece of a document, such as a function, a paragraph or a contract clause."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Embedding"}]},{"type":"text","text":": a list of numbers, also called a vector, that describes a piece of text, where similar meanings give similar numbers."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Encoder model"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the model that turns text into embeddings."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Cosine similarity"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the score that measures how close two embeddings are, where a high score means the two are very similar."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Vector database"}]},{"type":"text","text":": stores each embedding with its original text, and returns the closest matches to a query vector."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Test set"}]},{"type":"text","text":": a list of questions, where each question lists the chunks that a person marked as relevant."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Recall@k"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the share of all relevant chunks that appear in the top k results."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Precision@k"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the share of the top k chunks that are relevant."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"MRR (mean reciprocal rank)"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the average, over all questions, of 1 divided by the position of the first relevant chunk."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"nDCG@k"}]},{"type":"text","text":": a score that rewards relevant chunks near the top, where 1.0 means the best order."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"summary","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Summary"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"RAG keeps your data outside the model, and it fetches only the relevant part for each question. Text becomes embeddings. The embeddings live in a vector database, and a question finds its matches by similarity."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Remember the difference. The weights are the library, and the context window is the desk. Fine-tuning rearranges the library. RAG chooses what goes on the desk."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Next come tools: they are how a model stops reading and starts doing."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"references","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"References"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"-articles","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"📄 Articles"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://www.redhat.com/en/topics/ai/rag-vs-fine-tuning"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"RAG vs fine-tuning"}]},{"type":"text","text":": Red Hat's comparison, the long version of the section above"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://weaviate.io/blog/retrieval-evaluation-metrics"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Evaluation Metrics for Search and Recommendation Systems"}]},{"type":"text","text":": Recall, precision, MRR and nDCG, each with its formula and code"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"-videos","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"🎬 Videos"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://youtube.com/shorts/KBRvB_NDY-o?si=DIUHt8lihi0EzgxT"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"What is RAG?"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the whole idea, in one short video"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://youtube.com/shorts/24jqSMs10zE?si=zuhAbSZcFGkTKVfI"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Fine-Tuning vs RAG: Why Not Both?"}]},{"type":"text","text":": on using the two together"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"️-tools","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"🛠️ Tools"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://haystack.deepset.ai/"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Haystack"}]},{"type":"text","text":": RAG pipelines from ready-made parts"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://www.llamaindex.ai/"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"LlamaIndex"}]},{"type":"text","text":": connecting an LLM to your data"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"️-repositories","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"🗃️ Repositories"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/chroma-core/chroma"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"ChromaDB"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the easiest vector database to start with"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/facebookresearch/faiss"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"FAISS"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the search layer on its own"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/infiniflow/ragflow"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"RAGFlow"}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/HKUDS/LightRAG"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"LightRAG"}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/VectifyAI/PageIndex"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"PageIndex"}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/HKUDS/RAG-Anything"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"RAG-Anything"}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/arc53/DocsGPT"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"DocsGPT"}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/HKUDS/MiniRAG"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"MiniRAG"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/StarTrail-org/PixelRAG"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"PixelRAG"}]},{"type":"text","text":": complete RAG systems you can run as they are"}]}]}]}],"draft":null,"quiz":[{"question":"What is the main idea of Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)?","options":["Put all of your data into the context window for every question.","Keep the data outside the model and fetch only the relevant parts per question.","Train the model on your data so that it remembers the facts.","Make the context window larger so that all of your data fits in it."],"answer":1,"explain":"RAG keeps the data outside the model and puts only the relevant parts in the context for each question. Putting all the data in the context does not work, because the context window is a hard limit."},{"question":"A contract clause has the same meaning as the question, but almost no words in common. Why does retrieval with embeddings still find it?","options":["The search counts the words that the two texts have in common.","The LLM reads every clause in the database and picks the best one.","Similar meaning gives similar numbers, so the two vectors are close.","Cosine similarity gives a high score to texts of the same length."],"answer":2,"explain":"Embeddings catch meaning, not matching words, so texts with the same meaning get close vectors. Keyword search counts shared words, so it misses this clause."},{"question":"RAG has a setup part and an answering part. In the answering part, what happens after the question becomes an embedding?","options":["RAG breaks the source data into chunks and stores them in the database.","RAG fine-tunes the model on the closest chunks, then asks the question.","RAG sends the closest vectors to the LLM, which answers from the numbers.","RAG finds the closest chunks and puts their real text in the context."],"answer":3,"explain":"RAG searches the database for the closest chunks and puts their real text in the context. Breaking the data into chunks is setup, which happens once and again when the data changes."},{"question":"Your company's documents change every day. Why is RAG better than fine-tuning for these facts?","options":["Fine-tuning is slow and costly, so you cannot repeat it after every change.","Fine-tuning makes the context window smaller, so fewer documents fit in it.","Fine-tuning is good at teaching facts but bad at teaching a format.","RAG updates the model's weights faster than fine-tuning can update them."],"answer":0,"explain":"Fine-tuning takes hours or days and costs money, so you cannot repeat it each time a file changes. RAG changes what the model sees, not what it knows."},{"question":"Retrieval returns five chunks, and the only relevant one is third. What is the reciprocal rank?","options":["3","1/5","1/3","3/5"],"answer":2,"explain":"Reciprocal rank is 1 divided by the position of the first relevant chunk, so 1/3. MRR is the average of this number over all the questions in a test set."}]}